\end{table}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.65\textwidth]{figures/factorial_2x2.pdf}
\caption{$2{\times}2$ factorial comparison: normalization type (BN/LN) $\times$ gradient flow (ON/OFF). BN row: gradient flow reduces CAR by 4.2\,pp ($d{=}0.93$); LN row: 4.9\,pp ($n{=}1$, preliminary). All LN cells exceed Vanilla baseline. Cell~4 (Detach+LN) $n{=}1$; additional seeds in progress. Error bars: cross-seed SD where $n{\geq}2$.}
\label{fig:factorial}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Gradient Dynamics}

Gradient analysis (Figure~\ref{fig:gradient_analysis}, Appendix) examines the mechanism through which auxiliary heads affect safety. All gradient norms are computed with respect to the L1 action loss (action tokens only).

\textbf{Gradient orthogonality rules out direct conflict.} Action--auxiliary gradient cosine similarity is near zero across all encoder layers for both Full OCC and Random OCC, with conflict rates of ${\approx}$46\%. Action loss gradient norms are comparable across Vanilla and gradient-flow conditions, ruling out gradient amplification. Both patterns are identical for structured and random labels, consistent with the behavioral finding that spatial content did not detectably affect outcomes in our experiments. (Random OCC gradient data were obtained under the v1 codebase; the v2 gradient analysis reported here covers all four factorial conditions and Vanilla, each using a v2-trained checkpoint.)

\textbf{Mechanism remains open.} The observed effect pattern cannot be explained by gradient conflict (orthogonality rules it out) or gradient amplification (norms are comparable). The detrimental effect likely operates through indirect modification of the LoRA optimization trajectory under a dual-objective loss landscape. CKA analysis~\cite{kornblith2019similarity} confirms that encoder representations remain intact under all conditions (CKA $\geq$ 0.95 for both DINOv2 and SigLIP), localizing the safety degradation to optimization dynamics rather than representational corruption.

Additional observations (per-task breakdown, depth comparison, and temporal dynamics) are in Appendix~\ref{app:additional}.

