\title{Does Auxiliary Spatial Supervision Make Vision-Language-Action Models Safer?\\ An Empirical Investigation}

\author{
Anonymous Author(s)
}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuned Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models can achieve high task success yet remain behaviorally unsafe, with collision avoidance rates lagging task completion on safety-critical benchmarks.
We present a factorial study of twelve conditions varying five factors (gradient flow, label content, normalization type, spatial reference frame, and temporal extent) to isolate how auxiliary spatial supervision during VLA fine-tuning affects collision avoidance.
Our central finding is a \textbf{normalization-dependent gradient flow effect}. Under Batch Normalization (BN), gradient flow degrades safety: Full OCC BN achieves a 0.725 Collision Avoidance Rate versus 0.767 for Detach BN (Cohen's $d = -0.93$, large), falling below the no-auxiliary-head Vanilla baseline (0.745). Under Layer Normalization (LN), gradient flow is benign: Full OCC LN reaches 0.777, above Vanilla (Cohen's $d = +1.11$, large). Two large main effects drive this pattern: (1)~gradient flow under BN reduces CAR by 4.2\,pp ($d{=}0.93$), with BN + gradient flow as the sole configuration falling below the Vanilla baseline; (2)~LN provides a consistent 4--5\,pp safety advantage over BN ($d{\approx}1.03$, large), regardless of gradient flow. The BN + gradient flow cell is both the worst-performing and most unstable configuration ($\sigma{=}0.064$, ${\geq}\,2{\times}$ all other cells). Spatial label content (ground-truth occupancy, random binary labels, egocentric depth maps) shows no detectable effect on safety (observed under v1 codebase); the gradient pathway, not the spatial information, determines the outcome. Normalization choice is equally consequential: LayerNorm consistently provides a safety advantage over BatchNorm regardless of gradient flow configuration.
We hypothesize that this normalization dependence arises from BN's running statistics: auxiliary gradients may shift batch-level statistics in the shared encoder, a coupling absent in LN's per-token computation. Practical guidelines: detach auxiliary gradients under BN architectures; gradient flow is safe under LN; avoid combining BN with gradient flow.
\end{abstract}

\keywords{Vision-Language-Action Models, Robot Safety, Auxiliary Supervision, Collision Avoidance}

