\section{Codebase Sensitivity (v1 vs.\ v2)}
\label{app:codebase}

The five core factorial conditions were retrained under a revised codebase (v2) that fixed a batch-index resume bug affecting cutoff timing and restructured the auxiliary head pipeline (module initialization order, data loading). All model architectures, hyperparameters, LoRA configuration, and evaluation protocols are identical between v1 and v2. The Vanilla baseline (no auxiliary head) is unaffected by these changes, since the revision touches only auxiliary-head code paths.

\textbf{Behavioral shift.} Under v1, Full OCC achieved a mean CAR of 0.797 (3 seeds, per-task Table~\ref{tab:per_task}); under v2, the same condition yields 0.725 $\pm$ 0.064 (Table~\ref{tab:main_results}). Same-seed comparisons show shifts of up to 8.6\,pp, while Vanilla remains stable across codebases ($<$1.5\,pp). This sensitivity is confined to conditions with auxiliary heads and is itself informative: it indicates that auxiliary head training dynamics are fragile with respect to implementation details that would not normally be considered hyperparameters.

\textbf{Gradient analysis shift.} The v1 codebase exhibited a Detach gradient suppression artifact: Detach action-loss gradient norms were only $\sim$4\% of Vanilla norms across all encoder layers. In v2, this suppression disappears entirely (Detach/Vanilla ratio $\approx$ 1.29), indicating that the v1 pattern was a code-path artifact rather than a genuine effect of gradient detachment. The gradient conflict rate also shifted from $\sim$44\% (v1) to $\sim$46\% (v2), with v2 values closer to the theoretical expectation for orthogonal vectors.

\textbf{Effect pattern shift.} Under v1, the normalization $\times$ gradient flow pattern appeared as a crossover ($p{=}0.025$); under v2, both rows slope in the same direction, consistent with two additive main effects rather than an interaction. The crossover pattern in v1 is likely attributable to the Detach gradient suppression artifact described above, which differentially affected BN and LN conditions. All main-text analyses use v2 data exclusively; v1 results are retained in the appendix for completeness and to document the sensitivity.

\textbf{Implication.} The codebase sensitivity reinforces our central finding: auxiliary spatial heads introduce optimization fragility that implementation details can amplify or attenuate. This fragility is a further argument against adding auxiliary spatial heads for safety purposes, since the safety outcome depends on implementation choices that are difficult to specify, reproduce, or audit.

\section{Statistical Power Analysis}
\label{app:stat_power}

The $2{\times}2$ factorial design prioritizes breadth (12 conditions, 5 factors) over per-cell statistical power. For the BN row, the observed effect size is large (Cohen's $d{=}-0.93$), but with $n{=}3$ vs.\ $n{=}4$ seeds per cell, post-hoc power is approximately 0.3--0.4~\cite{cohen1988statistical}, meaning there is a 60--70\% probability of failing to detect a true effect of this magnitude at $\alpha{=}0.05$. We report this limitation transparently.

However, the study's evidential strength does not rest on any single pairwise comparison. The consistent gradient flow effect (both BN and LN rows show lower CAR under gradient flow ON than OFF) provides the primary evidence for the dual main effects, complemented by the large observed effect size (Cohen's $d{=}-0.93$) in the fully powered BN row. Seed expansion ($n{=}5$ per factorial cell) is underway to increase per-cell power.

\textbf{Formal $2{\times}2$ ANOVA.} Table~\ref{tab:anova} reports the factorial analysis. The interaction contrast is negligible ($F{=}0.02$, $p{=}0.89$, $\eta^2{=}0.003$), confirming that the factorial pattern reflects two additive main effects rather than a statistical interaction. Neither main effect reaches significance at $\alpha{=}0.05$ (gradient flow $p{=}0.388$, normalization $p{=}0.172$, both permutation tests), consistent with the limited per-cell sample sizes ($n{=}1$--$4$). Effect sizes, however, are medium-to-large: gradient flow $d{=}-0.60$ (Hedges' $g{=}0.55$, ON $<$ OFF), normalization $d{=}0.93$ (Hedges' $g{=}0.85$, LN $>$ BN; bootstrap 95\% CI $[-0.003, +0.085]$). The non-significant $p$-values reflect insufficient statistical power, not absent effects.

