\textbf{Effect pattern shift.} Under v1, the normalization $\times$ gradient flow pattern appeared as a crossover ($p{=}0.025$); under v2, both rows slope in the same direction, consistent with two additive main effects rather than an interaction. The crossover pattern in v1 is likely attributable to the Detach gradient suppression artifact described above, which differentially affected BN and LN conditions. All main-text analyses use v2 data exclusively; v1 results are retained in the appendix for completeness and to document the sensitivity.

\textbf{Implication.} The codebase sensitivity reinforces our central finding: auxiliary spatial heads introduce optimization fragility that implementation details can amplify or attenuate. This fragility is a further argument against adding auxiliary spatial heads for safety purposes, since the safety outcome depends on implementation choices that are difficult to specify, reproduce, or audit.

